 Most code reuses other code through libraries: collections of ready-made functions you call when you need them, the way you might call a plumber. You're in charge; the library waits to be asked. A framework reverses that relationship. It provides the overall structure of a program (how it starts, how it handles requests, how it stores data, how it draws its screens), and your code fills in the blanks, waiting to be called. Programmers call this inversion of control, or, more memorably, the Hollywood principle: don't call us, we'll call you.[^v1-i8-ioc] With a library, you write the program and borrow pieces. With a framework, the program is already written, in outline, and you're writing the parts it left for you.
 
-That makes a framework a kind of constitution for code. It decides the structure of power: what runs when, what may talk to what, where data must live and which decisions are left to you. And like constitutions, frameworks embody a theory. One of the oldest and most influential, a design pattern called model-view-controller, devised in the late 1970s at Xerox's research center, divides a program into three parts: the model, which holds the data and the rules; the view, which shows it; and the controller, which handles the user's actions.[^v1-i8-mvc] It's a separation of powers for software, Montesquieu's idea applied to code, and for the same reason: when one part can do everything, every change becomes dangerous.
-
