-The most ambitious version arrived in 2008, in the middle of a financial crisis, when someone writing under the name Satoshi Nakamoto published a nine-page paper describing Bitcoin, a digital currency with no central bank.[^v1-i8-bitcoin] Its key invention was the blockchain: a shared ledger of transactions, copied across thousands of computers, in which new records are bundled into blocks and each block is mathematically chained to the one before it, so that changing any old record would mean redoing all the work that came after it. Who gets to add the next block is settled by a competition that costs real energy to enter, which makes cheating more expensive than honesty. The result is a framework for agreement among strangers without anyone in charge, the Byzantine generals of Interlude 5, solved with electricity and arithmetic. Whether that agreement amounts to trust, or only to its imitation, is a question Interlude 18 will take up properly.
-
-In 2015, Ethereum extended the idea from money to programs: smart contracts, code stored on the blockchain that executes automatically when its conditions are met, with no court, bank or lawyer required. It was Lessig's "code is law," taken literally. The following year, the experiment met its first constitutional crisis. A fund called The DAO, governed entirely by smart contracts, raised about $150 million worth of cryptocurrency from thousands of investors. Within weeks, someone found a flaw in its code and drained roughly a third of the money.[^v1-i8-dao] The code had done exactly what it said. By the logic of "code is law," the transfer was legitimate. The Ethereum community decided otherwise: most of it agreed to rewrite the blockchain's history to return the funds, while a minority, insisting that the code must be sovereign, refused and continued on the original chain, which survives as Ethereum Classic. Code was law, right up until people decided it wasn't. Underneath every technical framework, it turns out, lies a human one.
