 ### Forks That Weigh Megabytes
 
-Open weights made a new kind of fork possible, and a trick made it cheap. Fine-tuning a whole model produces another full copy of its weights. But the change that adapting a model needs tends to be simple, and *low-rank adaptation*, LoRA, takes advantage of that: it freezes each original weight matrix and learns the change as the product of two thin ones. For a layer whose weights form a 4,096-by-4,096 grid, some 16.8 million numbers, a rank-8 adapter learns one matrix of 4,096 by 8 and another of 8 by 4,096, about 65,000 numbers, less than half a percent of the original. A whole adapter is a file of megabytes rather than gigabytes, which can be shared, swapped or stacked on the same base model, and training one fits on hardware a graduate student can afford.[^v1-i8-lora] So models acquired family trees. The Hub records which base model each upload descends from, and by the end of 2025 one family of open models, Alibaba's Qwen, had more than a hundred thousand recorded descendants: fine-tunes, adapters, quantized copies and merges of merges.[^v1-i8-qwen] Open source had always allowed forking. Now it happened by the hundred thousand, to minds.
